    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Bottom Line: **This is an exceptional, top-of-market offer.**\n\nFor a Google L6 Senior Product Manager in the Bay Area, median total compensation typically sits around **$500,000 \u2013 $560,000/year**. Your offer significantly outperforms market medians, places you in the **top 5\u201310% of L6 compensation bands**, and represents a major upgrade over your current Meta package.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Compensation Breakdown & Comparison\n\n#### **Your Current Meta Package (IC6)**\n* **Base:** $240,000\n* **Target Bonus:** ~$36,000 (15%)\n* **Equity:** ~$250,000/year (with ~$200,000 vesting over the next 12 months)\n* **Current Total Comp:** **~$526,000/year** (or ~$476,000 in next 12 months)\n\n---\n\n#### **Google L6 Offer Breakdown**\n* **Base Salary:** **$260,000** (Against the posted range of $183,000 \u2013 $271,000, this is near the upper ceiling)\n* **Target Bonus:** **$52,000** (20%)\n* **Sign-on Bonus:** **$200,000** (Specifically offsets your next 12 months of unvested Meta RSUs dollar-for-dollar)\n* **Equity (GSUs):** **$1,340,000 over 4 years** (A typical L6 initial grant is ~$600k\u2013$800k; $1.34M is an annualized $335,000/year)\n\n#### **Google\u2019s Front-Loaded Vesting Schedule**\nGoogle utilizes a front-loaded GSU vesting schedule for new hires:\n* **Year 1 (38%):** ~$509,200\n* **Year 2 (32%):** ~$428,800\n* **Year 3 (20%):** ~$268,000\n* **Year 4 (10%):** ~$134,000\n\n#### **Projected Earnings Comparison**\n\n| Metric | Meta (IC6) | Google L6 (Year 1) | Google L6 (4-Yr Annual Avg) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 |\n| **Bonus** | $36,000 | $52,000 | $52,000 |\n| **Equity Vesting** | $200,000 \u2013 $250,000 | $509,200 | $335,000 |\n| **Sign-On** | \u2014 | $200,000 | $50,000 *(amortized)* |\n| **Total Compensation** | **~$476k \u2013 $526k** | **~$1,021,200** | **~$697,000/year** |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n* **Walk Away?** **No.** The role is your top choice, the team is compelling, and the compensation represents a substantial financial step forward.\n* **Negotiate Aggressively?** **No.** Pushing aggressively without competing leverage carries real downside risk. You already have near-top-of-band base pay, an unusually large equity grant, and a sign-on bonus that completely bridges your unvested cliff.\n* **Recommended Move:** **Accept, or make a very light, polite closing ask.**\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Negotiate (If You Choose To)\n\nBecause you do not have a competing offer, any negotiation should be a **\"one-and-done closing ask\"** tied directly to your immediate commitment to sign.\n\n#### **Where There Is (and Isn't) Room:**\n* **Base ($260k):** Very little room ($271k is the absolute cap).\n* **Sign-on ($200k):** Already strong.\n* **Equity ($1.34M):** This is the component recruiters have the most discretion to round up.\n\n#### **What to Ask For:**\nAsk to round up the equity grant from **$1.34M to $1.4M\u2013$1.45M**, framed as the final piece to get you to sign immediately.\n\n#### **Sample Script:**\n> *\"Thank you so much for putting this offer together. I\u2019m thrilled about the team and the scope of the role, and Google is definitely my first choice.*  \n>  \n> *I want to be completely transparent: if we can round the equity grant to **$1.45M over 4 years**, I am ready to sign the offer letter today without hesitation. Is that something you can get approved to close this out?\"*\n\nIf the recruiter says no or pushes back, graciously accept the original offer.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Key Details to Confirm Before Signing\n1. **Sign-on Bonus Repayment Terms:** Check if the $200k sign-on has a 1-year or 2-year clawback window, and whether it is paid in one lump sum within your first 30\u201360 days.\n2. **Vesting Schedule:** Confirm that your GSU grant follows the standard **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** front-loaded schedule.\n3. **Refresher Cycle:** Google\u2019s equity front-loading means your base equity drops sharply in Years 3 and 4. Refresher grants (which vest over future years) are intended to stack and smooth out that drop, so ensure you understand the annual performance review timing for your first refresh grant.",
